    <name>
      <text>Effect of moving averages in SD</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>If the standard deviation of \(\displaystyle x_1,x_2,x_3,...,x_n\) is \({@given_stddev@}\), then what is the standard deviation of \(\displaystyle {@a@},\,  {@b@}, \,..., \, {@c@}?\)</p>
<p>The new standard deviation \(= \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To find the standard deviation of a new set of values obtained by applying a linear transformation to the original set, we can use the following formula:</p>
<p>If \( \displaystyle X = [x_1, x_2, x_3, ..., x_n]\) is the original set of values with standard deviation \( \sigma \), and \( \displaystyle  Y= [y_1, y_2, y_3, ..., y_n]\)  is the new set of values obtained by applying the linear transformation \(aX + b\), then the standard deviation of \(Y\) is given by:</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle  \sigma_y = \left|a \right| \cdot \sigma_x \)</p>
<p>where \(\sigma_y \) is the standard deviation of \(Y\), \(\sigma_x\) is the standard deviation of \(X\), and \( \displaystyle  \left|a \right|\) denotes the absolute value of \(a\).</p>
<p>In this case, we have the original set of values \( \displaystyle X = [x_1, x_2, x_3, ..., x_n]\) with a standard deviation of \({@given_stddev@}\).</p>
<p>We want to find the standard deviation of the new set of values \( \displaystyle  Y = [{@a@},\,  {@b@}, \,..., \, {@c@}]\).</p>
<p>Here, \( \displaystyle  a = {@transformation_factor@}\) and \(b = {@shift_factor@}\). Applying the formula, we have:</p>
<p>\(  \displaystyle  \sigma_y = \left| {@transformation_factor@} \right| \cdot \sigma_x ={@abs(transformation_factor)@} \cdot {@shift_factor@} = {@new_stddev@} \)</p>
<p>Therefore, the standard deviation of the new set of values \( \displaystyle  {@a@},\,  {@b@}, \,..., \, {@c@}\) is \( {@new_stddev@} \).
</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
